# S12.1 final review: authenticated fetch identity and writable mappings

## Findings

The remote fetches changed uid to `chatops` but still inherited that account's
Git and SSH configuration, allowing URL rewrites or SSH aliases. The worktree
barrier scanned cwd and file descriptors, but a process could close its file
descriptor while retaining a writable memory mapping to the same inode.

## Resolution

Both Application and Control releases are now supplied as root-owned mode
`0600`, out-of-band SHA-256-bound Git bundles over authenticated Tailscale SSH.
The advertised Application `main`, Control `control-main` and annotated Runtime
Freeze tag must be present before isolated local-only root import; the normal
freeze verifier then proves every exact commit, tree and artifact binding.
There is no server-side network Git operation, so no server `chatops` Git/SSH
configuration, alias, proxy, credential or known-hosts file is consulted.
The imported tag and its trusted-controller digest are sealed before backup;
the intentionally older canonical Control checkout does not need the new tag
before the deployment controller starts.

The existing `/proc` process scan now reads writable mappings in addition to
cwd and descriptor links. A Linux regression process closes its descriptor
after creating a writable mmap and is still detected by the barrier.
